sity of Southern Denmark"],"title":"2-hydroxyglutarate mediates whitening of brown adipocytes coupled to nuclear softening upon mitochondrial dysfunction","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-831226","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Background: Impaired lung function and fibrotic scarring of the lungs following severe COVID-19 infection are well-known manifestations. However, the risk factors and predisposing factors are still unknown. This study explored whether serological biomarkers for collagen synthesis associate with impaired lung function and fibrotic scarring after COVID-19 infection. Methods: In a prospective, observational cohort study involving patients hospitalized with COVID-19 requiring intensive care from June 2020 to December 2021, patients were followed up at 1, 3, 6, and 12 months after discharge. Lung function, diffusion capacity, and a panel of serological biomarkers for collagen-based fibrogenesis (PRO-C3, PRO-C6, and PRO-FIB) were measured. Additionally, a high-resolution CT scan of the lungs was performed at 3 and 12 months. Results: Thirty-four patients were included in the study. Twenty-seven (80%) were men (mean age 61 years). Most patients were former or active smokers (56%), while 44% were never smokers. Levels of both PRO-C3, PRO-C6, and PRO-FIB were higher 3 months after discharge compared to the normal range. The highest levels were measured 1 month after discharge, with PRO-C3 23.2 ng/ml, PRO-C6 15.9 ng/ml, and PRO-FIB 29.3 ng/ml. The levels of PRO-C3 declined up to 6 months after discharge and were hereafter stabilized, whereas the levels of PRO-C6 declined for the entire follow up period. Lung function improved during the first 6 months and then stabilized. Comparing lung function with PRO-C3 and PRO-C6 showed a positive correlation with lung function improving, while levels of the biomarkers declined. However, only PRO-C3 was found to be significantly associated with improvement in lung function 1 month after discharge. Conclusions: This study found that PRO-C3 and PRO-C6 are associated with changes in lung function after severe COVID-19 infection. High levels of both PRO-C6 and PRO-C3 were found up to 6 months after discharge in patients with impaired lung function, however, the values declined towards reference levels after 12 months.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Orholm Nielsen, Anne","Brændholt Rasmussen, Kirsten","Toft, Frederikke Bay","Nielsen, Henning Morris Bay","Hildebrandt, Thomas","Sloth, Carsten","Mayorca-Guiliani, Alejandro E.","Karsdal, Morten","Leeming, Diana Julie","Borg, Rikke"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-819735"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Biomarkers","Covid 19","PRO C3","PRO C6","Pulmonary fibrosis"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-ZEA"],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-NBIO"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-PR-NBIO","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"serologically assessed markers of fibroblast activity pro c000000000003 and pro c000000000006 and risk assessment of pulmonary fibrosis following severe covid 000000000019 infection","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/20018525.2025.2510032","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-819735","identifier":[{"id":"40452856","type":"pmid"},{"id":"105006901500","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC12123901","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-9b6bea43-0e0d-480a-878f-6e7777ee0869"],"keyword":["COVID-19","PRO-C3","PRO-C6","biomarkers","pulmonary fibrosis"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-3651BE260C21E536DAEFD7737C968508"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/20018525.2025.2510032","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/452996510/Serologically_assessed_markers_of_fibroblast_activity_PRO-C3_and_PRO-C6_and_risk_assessment_of_pulmonary_fibrosis_following_severe_COVID-19_infectio.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"region zealand","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-ZEA","l3nl":"Region Zealand","l3nn":"Region Zealand","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region Zealand","namevar":["Zealand University Hospital - Roskilde"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-ZEA","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Herlev and Gentofte","Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, No speciality"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"dk","key":"nordic bioscience","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-NBIO","l3nl":"Nordic Bioscience","l3nn":"Nordic Bioscience","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Nordic Bioscience","namevar":["Nordic Bioscience"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-NBIO","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Anne","key":"orholmnielsenpau","last":"Orholm Nielsen","name":"Orholm Nielsen, Anne","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Kirsten","key":"brændholtrasmussenpau","last":"Brændholt Rasmussen","name":"Brændholt Rasmussen, Kirsten","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Frederikke Bay","key":"toftpau","last":"Toft","name":"Toft, Frederikke Bay","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","first":"Henning Bay","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"0f8a6982-69ab-45ef-88a4-6bb61c6978f5","name":"Nielsen, Henning Morris Bay","name_org":"Nielsen, Henning Bay","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Thomas","key":"hildebrandtpau","last":"Hildebrandt","name":"Hildebrandt, Thomas","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Carsten","key":"slothpau","last":"Sloth","name":"Sloth, Carsten","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Alejandro E.","key":"mayorcaguilianipau","last":"Mayorca-Guiliani","name":"Mayorca-Guiliani, Alejandro E.","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Morten","key":"karsdalpau","last":"Karsdal","name":"Karsdal, Morten","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Diana Julie","key":"leemingpau","last":"Leeming","name":"Leeming, Diana Julie","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","first":"Rikke","key":"borgpau","last":"Borg","locid":"19f3b871-b1fc-4653-aee0-be709d2a16c9","name":"Borg, Rikke","orcid":"0000-0002-2283-3578","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40452856"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1080/20018525.2025.2510032","issn":["20018525"],"issue":"1","journal":"European Clinical Respiratory Journal","paperid":"2510032","status":"p","title":"European Clinical Respiratory Journal","volume":"12"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105006901500"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Private Research","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Nordic Bioscience","Private Research","Region Zealand","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-9b6bea43-0e0d-480a-878f-6e7777ee0869"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Serologically assessed markers of fibroblast activity (PRO-C3 and PRO-C6) and risk assessment of pulmonary fibrosis following severe COVID-19 infection","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-819735","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Smart contracts executed on blockchains are interactive programs where external actors generate events that trigger function invocations. Events can be emitted by participants asynchronously. However, some functionalities should be restricted to participants inhabiting specific roles in the system, which might be dynamically adjusted while the system evolves. We argue that current smart contract languages adopting imperative programming paradigms require additional complicated access control code. Furthermore, smart contracts are often developed and evolved independently and cannot share a joint access control policy. This makes it challenging to ensure the correctness of access control properties and to maintain correctness when the contracts are adapted. We propose using dynamic condition response (DCR) graphs for role-based and declarative access control for smart contracts and techniques for test-driven modelling and refinement of DCR graphs to support the safe design and evolution of smart contracts. We show that they allow for capturing and visualizing a form of dynamic access control where access rights evolve as the contract state progresses. Their use supports the straightforward declaration of access control rights, improved code auditing, test-driven modelling, and safe evolution of smart contracts and improves users' understanding.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Xu, Yibin","Slaats, Tijs","Düdder, Boris","Hildebrandt, Thomas T.","Van Cutsem, Tom"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-810693"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Belgium"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","KU Leuven"],"keyword":["Process oriented programming","Refinement","Test driven modelling"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"safe design and evolution of smart contracts using dynamic condition response graphs to model generic role based behaviors","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/smr.2730","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-810693","identifier":[{"id":"85205281997","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-ab92923d-fb91-44bd-8452-1257d13b1931"],"keyword":["process-oriented programming","refinement","role-based programming","safe evolution","smart contract programming","smart contract security","test-driven modelling"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-0D7461E6E48F60239602C802CE077570"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/448542205/Safe_design_and_evolution.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/smr.2730","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Software, Data, People & Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"be","key":"ku leuven","loc":["Belgium","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"KU Leuven","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Yibin","key":"xupau","last":"Xu","locid":"08a435ed-3aab-4eec-88f1-c4e8c74b7155","name":"Xu, Yibin","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Tijs","key":"slaatspau","last":"Slaats","locid":"85f00008-e9c6-439c-aae5-e5bd725f2099","name":"Slaats, Tijs","orcid":"0000-0001-6244-6970","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Boris","key":"düdderpau","last":"Düdder","locid":"26641b70-8c76-4150-ae85-086cfa904763","name":"Düdder, Boris","orcid":"0000-0002-0241-7729","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Thomas","key":"troelshildebrandtpau","last":"Troels Hildebrandt","locid":"c1d69914-5f7a-4f0b-8ffb-766693004328","name":"Hildebrandt, Thomas T.","name_org":"Troels Hildebrandt, Thomas","orcid":"0000-0002-7435-5563","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Tom","key":"vancutsempau","last":"Van Cutsem","name":"Van Cutsem, Tom","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1002/smr.2730","issn":["20477473","20477481"],"issn_org":["20477481"],"issue":"1","journal":"Journal of Software: Evolution and Process","paperid":"e2730","status":"p","title":"Journal of Software: Evolution and Process","title_org":"Journal of software: Evolution and Process","volume":"37"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85205281997"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-ab92923d-fb91-44bd-8452-1257d13b1931"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Safe design and evolution of smart contracts using dynamic condition response graphs to model generic role-based behaviors","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-810693","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Background: Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) is the most common form of lymphoma. European guidelines recommend FDG-PET/CT for staging and end of treatment (EOT) response assessment, mid-treatment response assessment is optional. We compared the Lugano classification and PET Response Criteria In Solid Tumours (PERCIST) for FDG-PET/CT response assessment in DLBCL head-to-head. Methods: We retrospectively included patients with DLBCL who underwent first-line R-CHOP(-like) therapy (2013−2020). Interim and EOT FDG-PET/CT response were reevaluated using the Lugano classification and PERCIST. Response was dichotomized into complete metabolic response (CMR) versus non-CMR (interim and EOT) and responders versus nonresponders (interim only). The cutoff for nonresponse at interim was a Deauville score of 5 (DS5) with the Lugano classification and a partial metabolic response with ≤66% reduction in SULpeak using PERCIST (PERCIST66). Results: In multivariable Cox regression (N = 170), DS5 at interim, PERCIST66 at interim, non-CMR at EOT with the Lugano classification and non-CMR at EOT with PERCIST were predictive of progression-free survival (PFS). The Lugano classification and PERCIST agreed perfectly at interim and EOT and with 98.4% for the identification of nonresponders at interim. The accuracy for predicting events within 2 years of diagnosis was 84.2% for DS-5 at interim, 87.6% for PERCIST66 at interim, 86% for non-CMR with the Lugano classification at EOT and 83.3% for non-CMR with PERCIST at EOT. Conclusion: The Lugano classification and PERCIST were equally predictive of PFS. Nonresponse at interim and non-CMR at EOT were predictive of poor PFS with comparable accuracy for predicting events within 2 years.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Nielsen, Nicklas B.","Gerke, Oke","Nielsen, Anne Lerberg","Juul-Jensen, Karen","Larsen, Thomas Stauffer","Møller, Michael Boe","Hildebrandt, Malene Grubbe"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-714803"],"filters":{"added":"2024-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Gerke, Oke"],"keyword":["Accuracy","Agreement","DLBCL","EOT","Interim","Predictive ability","Progression Free Survival","Response evaluation"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a retrospective head to head comparison of the lugano classification and percist for fdg pet ct response assessment in diffuse large b cell lymphoma","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/cpf.12851","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This investigation received financial support from the Danish Cancer Society and the Center for Personalized Response Monitoring in Oncology, Odense University Hospital, Denmark."],"id":"S01-000.00-714803","identifier":[{"id":"37501554","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85167708459","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-e9dca8b3-5134-4352-8ef9-0d4397200ef5"],"keyword":["DLBCL","EOT","accuracy","agreement","interim","predictive ability","progression-free survival","response evaluation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-CBC81814DD2806CFADB3F5F0ED0E0257"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/246196141/Clin_Physio_Funct_Imaging_2023_Nielsen_A_retrospective_head_to_head_comparison_of_the_Lugano_classification_and.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/cpf.12851","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research Unit OPEN (Odense)","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of Clinical Physiology and Nuclear Medicine (Odense)","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of Oncology (Odense)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nicklas B.","key":"nielsennicklasbpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"f43685de-1614-4bb6-a444-97324e1cf384","name":"Nielsen, Nicklas B.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oke","key":"gerkeokepau","last":"Gerke","locid":"596baeb1-6754-4d53-948f-30470dd1d976","name":"Gerke, Oke","orcid":"0000-0001-6335-3303","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anne L.","key":"nielsenannelerbergpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"ba5686a6-f441-4c7a-9348-f0501da77640","name":"Nielsen, Anne Lerberg","name_org":"Nielsen, Anne L.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Karen","key":"juuljensenkarenpau","last":"Juul-Jensen","locid":"bbcd089b-e5fb-4195-b1cd-6b31fe740f04","name":"Juul-Jensen, Karen","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas S.","key":"larsenthomasstaufferpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"dd2de49b-c3d9-49cb-bb8d-e92119e0b2d1","name":"Larsen, Thomas Stauffer","name_org":"Larsen, Thomas S.","orcid":"0000-0002-8418-6708","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael B.","key":"møllermichaelboepau","last":"Møller","locid":"edd876e8-0651-4c8f-8f0f-95f4519e1654","name":"Møller, Michael Boe","name_org":"Møller, Michael B.","orcid":"0000-0003-2041-3630","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Malene G.","key":"hildebrandtmalenegrubbepau","last":"Hildebrandt","locid":"89ec551d-ddfe-45e8-983a-f2bbc54f8a0e","name":"Hildebrandt, Malene Grubbe","name_org":"Hildebrandt, Malene G.","orcid":"0000-0003-2720-9018","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["37501554"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/cpf.12851","issn":["14750961","1475097X"],"issn_org":["14750961"],"issue":"1","journal":"Clinical Physiology and Functional Imaging","pages":"70-78","publisher":"Wiley","status":"p","title":"Clinical Physiology and Functional Imaging","volume":"44"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85167708459"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-e9dca8b3-5134-4352-8ef9-0d4397200ef5"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"A retrospective head-to-head comparison of the Lugano classification and PERCIST for FDG-PET/CT response assessment in diffuse large B-cell lymphoma","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-714803","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"PURPOSE: The aim of this study was to evaluate one-year outcomes of lower versus higher oxygenation targets in intensive care unit (ICU) patients with coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) and severe hypoxaemia. METHODS: We conducted pre-planned analyses of one-year mortality and health-related quality of life (HRQoL) in the Handling Oxygenation Targets in COVID-19 trial. The trial randomised 726 ICU patients with COVID-19 and hypoxaemia to partial pressure of arterial oxygen targets of 8 kPa (60 mmHg) versus 12 kPa (90 mmHg) during ICU stay up to 90 days, including readmissions. HRQoL was assessed using EuroQol visual analogue scale (EQ-VAS) and 5-level 5-dimension questionnaire (EQ-5D-5L). Outcomes were analysed in the intention-to-treat population. Non-survivors were assigned the worst possible score (zero), and multiple imputation was applied for missing EQ-VAS values. RESULTS: We obtained one-year vital status for 691/726 (95.2%) of patients and HRQoL data for 642/726 (88.4%). At one year, 117/348 (33.6%) of patients in the lower-oxygenation group had died compared to 134/343 (39.1%) in the higher-oxygenation group (adjusted risk ratio: 0.85; 98.6% confidence interval (CI) 0.66-1.09; p = 0.11). Median EQ-VAS was 50 (interquartile range, 0-80) versus 40 (0-75) (adjusted mean difference: 4.8; 98.6% CI  - 2.2 to 11.9; p = 0.09) and EQ-5D-5L index values were 0.61 (0-0.81) in the lower-oxygenation group versus 0.43 (0-0.79) (p = 0.20) in the higher-oxygenation group, respectively. CONCLUSION: Among adult ICU patients with COVID-19 and severe hypoxaemia, one-year mortality results were most compatible with benefit of the lower oxygenation target, which did not appear to result in more survivors with poor quality of life.","author_count":19,"contributors":["Crescioli, Elena","Nielsen, Frederik Mølgaard","Bunzel, Anne-Marie Gellert","Broberg Eriksen, Anne Sofie","Siegemund, Martin","Poulsen, Lone Musaeus","Andreasen, Anne Sofie","Bestle, Morten Heiberg","Iversen, Susanne Andi","Brøchner, Anne Craveiro","Grøfte, Thorbjørn","Hildebrandt, Thomas","Laake, Jon Henrik","Kjær, Maj-Brit Nørregaard","Lange, Theis","Perner, Anders","Lass Klitgaard, Thomas","Schjørring, Olav Lilleholt","Rasmussen, Bodil Steen"],"cuid":["S03-093.82-766465"],"filters":{"added":"2024-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Crescioli, Elena"],"country":["Norway","Switzerland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Oslo University Hospital","University Hospital Basel","University Hospital of Basel","University of Basel","University of Oslo"],"keyword":["Aged","COVID 19 mortality","Covid 19","Female","Humans","Hypoxia mortality","Intensive Care Units","Intensive Care Units statistics numerical data","Male","Middle Aged","Mortality","Oxygen Inhalation Therapy","Oxygen Inhalation Therapy methods","Oxygen blood","Quality of Life","SARS CoV 2"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"long term mortality and health related quality of life with lower versus higher oxygenation targets in intensive care unit patients with covid 000000000019 and severe hypoxaemia","src":["aau","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00134-024-07613-2","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Open access funding provided by Aalborg University Hospital. The trial was funded by the Danish Ministry of Education and Science and Supported by the Research Council at Aalborg University Hospital. Funders had no role in the design and conduct of the trial; collection, management, analysis, and interpretation of data; preparation, review, or approval of the manuscript; nor the decision to submit the manuscript for publication. The Department of Intensive Care at Rigshospitalet (AP, MNK) received funding from Sygeforsikringen \\u201CDanmark\\u201D, The Novo Nordisk Foundation, the Ehrenreich Foundation, the Becketts Foundation, and AM-Pharma. TL has served in Data and Safety Monitoring Board for Novo Nordisk and Leo Pharma, respectively: the studies were unrelated to the present study."],"id":"S03-093.82-766465","identifier":[{"id":"PMC11446942","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"39235624","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85203269733","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-00f464c1-b215-4113-8eff-af3da71e7dde","ku-4e6b0bee-730c-4429-8546-414177fdf4b4","sdu-faa591ce-57d2-4a76-8cbc-05101c9250e6"],"keyword":["Aged","COVID-19","COVID-19/mortality","Female","Humans","Hypoxia/mortality","Intensive Care Units/statistics & numerical data","Intensive care units","Male","Middle Aged","Mortality","Oxygen Inhalation Therapy/methods","Oxygen inhalation therapy","Oxygen/blood","Quality of life","SARS-CoV-2"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-474B43F4D20FA78E049F5654ADF71444"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/747014809/Crescioli_et_al._2024_._Long-term_mortality_and_health-related_quality_of_life_with_lower_versus_higher_oxygenation_targets_in_intensive_care_unit_patients_with_COVID-19_and_severe_hypoxaemia.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/278645462/Open_Access_Version.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00134-024-07613-2","version":"Published Version"}],"org